      "response": "## Investigation report\n\nCitation integrity checked against saved evidence. Human review is required to assess the findings.\n\n### 1. Correctness · Low\n\nThe code imports Streamlit as st and defines a function load\\_rows decorated with @st\\.cache\\_data\\.\n\nSource: User supplied reproduction code · user\\_code · ev\\_90c06e99e5b706b6f11b8ab163d95e21\n\n> import streamlit as st\n> \n> @st\\.cache\\_data\n> def load\\_rows\\(\\):\n>     return \\[1, 2, 3\\]\n> \n> st\\.write\\(load\\_rows\\(\\)\\)\n\n### 2. Correctness · Low\n\nThe function load\\_rows returns a static list \\[1, 2, 3\\] and its result is displayed using st\\.write\\.\n\nSource: User supplied reproduction code · user\\_code · ev\\_90c06e99e5b706b6f11b8ab163d95e21\n\n> import streamlit as st\n> \n> @st\\.cache\\_data\n> def load\\_rows\\(\\):\n>     return \\[1, 2, 3\\]\n> \n> st\\.write\\(load\\_rows\\(\\)\\)\n\n### Missing information\n\n- Whether the Streamlit runtime has caching enabled or overridden by environment variables or command‑line flags\\.\n- Any error messages that might arise if the cache decorator is misused \\(e\\.g\\., CacheError or serialization issues\\)\\.\n\n### Proposed next steps\n\n- Perform a focused check that the @st\\.cache\\_data decorator is active: verify that the Streamlit configuration does not disable data caching \\(e\\.g\\., check the 'global\\.cacheData' setting\\) and confirm that repeated calls to load\\_rows do not re\\-execute the function body\\.\n",
